  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1233 PM MDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nPrecipitation has finally come to an end across eastern Idaho. \nThe area of low pressure that impacted us the last few days is \nover southeast Utah. This low continues to weaken and move \neastward today with high pressure building in from the west \nover southeast Idaho.\n\nThere are some lingering clouds across our eastern areas\ncurrently but most areas have cleared out. With high pressure,\nclear skies, and lingering moisture expect patchy fog tomorrow \nmorning. Expect dry conditions through Monday with a gradual \nwarming trend under high pressure. Winds will remain light \nthrough Sunday. For lower elevations, highs today will be in the\n50s for most and we return to the low 60s for Friday afternoon.\nExpect mid 60s by Sunday and Monday. \n\nOverall, the models are showing a system starting to move into \nthe area by early next Tuesday. Though, models continue to \ndisagree on exact timing. Some models hold off the system until \nlater next week. If models stay on the same track, expect winds \nto pick up by Monday afternoon. Currently models show good \nprecipitation chances Tuesday and Wednesday. Currently, overall \naccumulation looks light to moderate, 0.10 to 0.25 for valleys \nand 0.25 to 0.5 for mountains.",
